    <heading level="1">11.4. Self-Check Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Show that the series $$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\sqrt{n^{2} + 1}}{2n + 3}$$ is divergent.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Here,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n}} = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\sqrt{n^{2} + 1}}{2n + 3}}} \\ {\phantom{\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n}} = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\sqrt{1 + \frac{1}{n^{2}}}}{2 + \frac{3}{n}} = \frac{1}{2} \neq 0}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>so by the divergence test, the series diverges.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the integral test to determine whether the series $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n\ln n}$$ converges or diverges.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Here, $$a_{n} = 1 \slash (n\ln n)$$ , so we consider the function $$f{(x)} = 1 \slash (x\ln x)$$ . Observe that $$f$$ is continuous and positive on $$\left\lbrack 2,\infty) \right.$$ . Next, we compute</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{f^{\prime}{(x)}} = \frac{(x\ln x){(0)} - {(1)}(\ln x + 1)}{\left\lbrack x(\ln x) \right\rbrack^{2}}} \\ {\phantom{f^{\prime}{(x)}} = {- \frac{\ln x + 1}{\left\lbrack x(\ln x) \right\rbrack^{2}}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Note that $$f{(x)} &lt; 0$$ if $$\ln x &gt; - 1$$ , that is, if $$x &gt; 1 \slash e$$ . This shows that $$f$$ is decreasing on $$\left\lbrack 2,\infty) \right.$$ . Therefore, we may use the integral test.</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\int_{2}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{x\ln x}\mathit{dx}}} = {\lim\limits_{b\rightarrow\infty}{\int_{2}^{b}{\frac{1}{x\ln x}\mathit{dx}}}}} \\ {\phantom{\int_{2}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{x\ln x}\mathit{dx}}} = {\lim\limits_{b\rightarrow\infty}{\lbrack\ln(\ln x)\rbrack}_{2}^{b}}} \\ {\phantom{\int_{2}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{x\ln x}\mathit{dx}}} = {\lim\limits_{b\rightarrow\infty}\lbrack\ln(\ln b) - \ln(\ln 2)\rbrack}} \\ {\phantom{\int_{2}^{\infty}{\frac{1}{x\ln x}\mathit{dx}}} = \infty} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>and we conclude that $$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n\ln n}$$ diverges.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
